WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:03.870
[suspenseful magical music]

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:12.846
[dramatic music]

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.683
I'm now more
than 230 miles west

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.418
of the fortress of Jiayuguan.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.028
I'm here in the Kumtag Desert.

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:31.131
It's one of the
harshest environments

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.866
I've ever been in in my life.

00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:34.300
Very little grows here.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:39.372
The temperatures are
literally below freezing.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.275
It's a hell of a place to
imagine finding archeology.

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:47.447
Sarah's guiding me to
faint traces of what

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:50.350
looks like an even
older wall that she

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:52.018
spotted on the satellite data.

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:55.155
[epic adventurous music]

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.425
If I get up here, I think
I can get my first glimpse.

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.836
Wow, this is cool.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:14.107
And it's not exactly
what I expected.

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:17.844
And it looks kind of primitive.

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.078
I want to see more.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:19.846
I want to get up closer.

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:30.023
Here, in the middle of nowhere,
a stretch of ancient wall.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:32.992
And it looks very different
from anything I've seen so far.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.700
This is a very distinctive
kind of material.

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:45.905
We have rammed earth, packed
down, with a reed foundation.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:46.940
These are reeds.

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:49.476
This is actually a
type of thick grass

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.211
pressed in there to
serve as a foundation

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:54.581
to hold the wall stable.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.749
This framework is
an ingenious way

00:01:56.816 --> 00:02:01.287
of building even with the
desert's loose sand and gravel.

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:06.826
But if the Ming didn't
build this, then who did?

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:10.830
To find out, I'm meeting Jang
Joon Min, the head of a team

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:13.833
from the Gansu Institute
of Relics and Archaeology,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:14.968
who are digging for answers.

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:19.138
Wow, this is so cool.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:21.374
Tell me what you have here.

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:22.642
[non-english speech]

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:25.912
TRANSLATOR: These are two
arrowheads we found nearby.

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:29.449
One is bronze, and one is iron.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:32.118
You can see, it's still sharp.

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:35.054
I've never seen this shape
to an arrowhead before.

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:39.626
You know, where it's basically
three-sided like this.

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:41.194
Is that common?

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:42.395
[non-english speech]

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:43.897
TRANSLATOR: We found lots
of these along the wall

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:45.331
in this area.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:49.602
These arrowheads are very
typical of the Han Dynasty.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.505
[mysterious music]

00:02:52.572 --> 00:02:54.474
[dramatic music]

00:02:54.541 --> 00:03:00.313
1,500 years before the Ming,
before the Roman Empire formed

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:05.685
in the West and Cleopatra
ruled Ancient Egypt,

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:08.221
the Han Dynasty rose to power.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:15.395
They oversaw a golden
age of art, culture,

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.863
and economic prosperity.

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:21.267
They're so well preserved.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:22.268
I'm just amazed.

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:23.836
Look at that edge.

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:25.171
It's just incredible.

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:30.009
The form is just so
clear and beautiful.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:33.246
These tiny arrowheads
reveal something momentous--

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:44.090
that the wall here is
more than 2,000 years old.

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:48.962
It's an even earlier Great
Wall of China that once

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:54.701
stretched for over 6,000 miles.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.670
Much of the famous
Ming Dynasty wall

00:03:57.737 --> 00:04:06.479
was simply built right
on top 1,500 years later.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.882
Battered by desert winds
for over two millennia, what

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:12.885
remains of this ancient
stretch of Great Wall

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:18.391
are just fragments of what
was once a mighty barrier.

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:20.760
It's kind of hard, just looking
at these ruined sections

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:24.130
of the wall, to get a sense of
what it looked like originally.

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:26.699
So using historical sources
and archaeological evidence,

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:28.635
we've made this
pretty phenomenal

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.870
digital reconstruction
of what the wall

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:33.272
might have looked like.

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:34.807
It's just amazing.

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:36.242
Whoa.

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:37.210
Look at that.

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:39.646
[dramatic music]

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:41.881
When it was first
built, the wall would

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.750
have stood up to 20 feet high.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:47.987
This thing was enormous.

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:51.557
I can only imagine being part
of an invading nomad army,

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:55.662
and coming up against
this wall, and thinking,

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:57.230
maybe it's better
to just go home,

00:04:57.296 --> 00:05:00.967
because this was formidable.

